P0-5 — Tributary presents its data-plane token to TimeLakeDB
2026-08-10 · tributary (release) -> timelakedb-da (TIMELAKE_DATA_AUTH=required)
================================================================================

THE POINT. TimeLakeDB's data plane now authenticates by token (SEC-4
phased). A shipper that cannot present one is not deployable against a
node running required mode. Tributary now sources a bearer token, sends
it on every write, and — the part that is easy to get wrong — never lets
it reach a log line.

WHERE THE TOKEN COMES FROM. `TRIBUTARY_TOKEN` (env, 12-factor) or
`[output].token_file` (a Kubernetes secret mount / systemd credential),
env winning. Deliberately NOT an inline config field: a secret in a
committed TOML is a secret leaked. Same split as TimeLakeDB's own
TIMELAKE_ENCRYPTION_KEY / _KEY_FILE, and the same seam a Vault backend
(ROADMAP §4) slots into later.

DRILLED END TO END against a real required-mode node
(bench/drill-p05.sh, run from a container on the node's network):

  10/10 checks passed

  correct token (scope=write, db=logs):
    - all 20,000 lines land in the table — exact count
    - agent read exactly the corpus
    - startup logged authenticated=true (no secret in the line)
  wrong token (tldb_definitely_not_valid):
    - zero rows reached the node
    - the unauthorized counter fired (6 — one per failed batch/retry)
    - the failure is reported AS AUTH ("TimeLakeDB rejected the token"),
      not as a generic transport error
    - the data was SPOOLED to the durable on-disk queue (4 segments),
      not dropped — a bad token loses nothing, it just stops flowing
  no token at all:
    - zero rows reached the node
    - startup logged authenticated=false
  redaction:
    - the token value appears in NONE of the agent's logs

  Reads in required mode need a token too, so the drill's own COUNT(*)
  verification uses a SEPARATE read-scoped token — which also exercises
  scope separation: the write token Tributary holds cannot read the
  database back, exactly as intended for a shipper credential.

REDACTION, TWO LAYERS.
  - `auth::Secret` wraps the token; its Debug and Display both print a
    placeholder, so `tracing::error!(?token)` or `{token}` cannot leak
    it. The bytes are reachable only through `.expose()`, the one
    conspicuous call site.
  - The Authorization header is built once and marked
    `set_sensitive(true)`, so reqwest's own diagnostics keep it out of
    connection traces — belt and suspenders.

WHY A BAD TOKEN IS NOT FATAL. Tributary's existing contract is "never
drop": a failed ship spools to the durable queue and retries. A 401 is
the same — the data is preserved, the agent alarms loudly and keeps
trying, and fixing the token (and restarting) drains the queue. A
distinct ShipError::Unauthorized keeps a bad credential from being
bisected like a poison line or hidden as a transport blip.

BUILD. tributary 51 tests (+6 auth: secret never prints, env-wins,
file-trimmed, empty-env-falls-through, missing-is-none, empty-file-is-
loud), clippy clean, fmt clean. reqwest is rustls-tls (no OpenSSL).
